WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:06.000
Who was looking. Five projects on how records, media and games shape what we know about nature, and what people do for it. Tarun K. Verma, conservation social scientist.

00:06.200 --> 00:16.000
Where the question began: Ranthambhore, 2021. For my M.Sc., I read 1,341 enforcement records from Ranthambhore Tiger Reserve. Recorded offences fell from 225 in 2019 to 98 in 2020. The reading was not that offending had stopped, but that cases were no longer being registered once COVID-19 arrived.

00:16.200 --> 00:28.000
01 of 05. UNEP Tide Turners Plastic Challenge, impact evaluation, 2024 to 2025. A youth programme on plastic that works like a game, from Entry to Leader to Champion. Delivered in India by WWF-India and the Centre for Environment Education, and evaluated by the University of Oxford and ATREE. 664 students in the trial, 55% more household recycling, 56 interviews.

00:28.000 --> 00:36.000
What the platform could not show: in Barmer, a teacher climbed a tree in her school yard to find enough phone signal to register her students. Why it matters: it showed where the programme worked and who it could not reach.

00:36.200 --> 00:54.000
02 of 05. WildTrace, an open atlas of illegal wildlife trade in animals and plants. Every seizure, arrest and conviction is graded by the strength of its evidence, and set against trade routes from CITES records. 1,285 graded cases in 59 countries as of September 2026. Why it matters: journalists, researchers and small NGOs can see how strong the evidence is.

00:54.200 --> 01:12.000
03 of 05. Protected Area Update, with Kalpavriksh, 2022 to 2024. The newsletter has reported from India's parks since the 1990s. At IIT Bombay I built its geospatial database and sketched how the website's map should work. Over 6,000 reports, more than 1,000 coded by me. Why it matters: three decades of local conservation news are now searchable and mapped for anyone.

01:12.200 --> 01:28.000
04 of 05. Sift QDA, a free qualitative analysis app for Windows. It codes interviews, PDFs and surveys, searches them by meaning, and works with NVivo, ATLAS.ti and MAXQDA projects. Why it matters: students and NGOs can do careful research without a licence, and sensitive interviews never leave their machine.

01:28.200 --> 01:42.000
05 of 05. Kosma, a privacy-first astrology engine. The AI may only interpret what two independent chart factors support, and everything else is withheld and listed as withheld. Why it matters: an AI that says what it cannot support.

01:42.200 --> 01:54.500
Open, citable, free. Who was looking: so that the people a record leaves out are counted too. tarunverma.com
